Ok, this solves my doubt.

I considered to use Poisson, but I don't think I can consider my response as Poisson distributed, I've a well defined number of trials which I can't ignore.

Thanks!

On Wednesday, May 1, 2019 at 8:58:57 AM UTC-7, Helpdesk wrote:

> you can add an offset using
>
>         y ~ ... + offset(var) + ...
>
> and then 'var' will be added as a constant in the formula.
>
> about the second question: have you considered to view y_ij as Poisson
> instead?  then the expose time is just there in the mean, see 'E' in
> the poisson definition.
>
> H
>
> On Wed, 2019-05-01 at 08:31 -0700, marcanto...@gmail.com wrote:
> > Hello list,
> >
> > I am modelling my data with a binomial outcome:
> >
> > yi,j ∼ Bin(pi,j , ni,j )
> > logit(pi,j ) = Xi,j + β + zi,j
> >
> > where n is the number of success and p the number of trials.
> >
> > Now, both n and p in my dataset are recorded at non constant
> > intervals, and I suspect that the proportion of success is somewhat
> > related with the exposure time.
> > I'd therefore like to add an offset term in my model, however I am
> > not quite sure how the offset term would interact with the trials in
> > the left hand side of the binomial likelihood in INLA.
> >
> > Can anyone explain this to me?
> >
> > Thanks
