> > >
> > > On Thursday, May 2, 2019 at 12:55:19 PM UTC+2, Helpdesk wrote:
> > > > the predictions from the fitted values are computed from the
> > > > linear
> > > > predictor using like exp(linear.predictor), if the link
> > function is
> > > > the
> > > > 'log'  (and the appropriate flag is given to do so, link=...)
> > > >
> > > > If the linear predictor contains random effects, then those
> > are
> > > > included in the fitted values. Often, is the random effects are
> > of
> > > > the
> > > > 'classical style', like model="iid", then their mean is zero
> > so
> > > > the
> > > > predictions with and without the random effects will mostly
> > differ
> > > > in
> > > > the variance (although E(exp(...))  depends also on the
> > variance
> > > > of
> > > > ...).
> > > >
> > > > You can easily compute the predictions only using the fixed
> > effect
> > > > if
> > > > you want to, either by using posterior samples, as you mention,
> > or
> > > > using linear combinations (see the FAQ page).
> > > >
> > > > If you want to predict the outcome of the zeroinflated output,
> > then
> > > > its
> > > > much easier to stay with the posterior sample stuff. (see also
> > > > inla.posterior.sample.eval()  which simplify life...)
> > > >
> > > > Best
> > > > H
